The entries of an account
19-the-master-files
Two kinds, which K switches between. The VOC entries of the account under the cursor in Accounts are listed in name order, each with its type (V verb, K keyword, F file, Q pointer to a file in another account, R pointer to a record, PA or PH paragraph, S stored sentence, X text) and what it points at. The dictionary items are those of every file the VOC points at, by the dictionary path of the file pointer, each with its file, id, type (D, I, E, L, PH, X) and column heading. The box under the lists shows the entry under the cursor field by field.
A > in the second column means the target lies outside this account: in SDSYS (the shipped pointers lead to @SDSYS/voc.dic and the like) or in another account. For a VOC entry the pointer itself is the account's own record and is edited like any other; what it points at is never edited from this page. A dictionary item marked > lives in another account's dictionary and is only read through this account's pointer; it is edited in the account it lives in.
/ keeps what holds a part you type, in the id, and in a dictionary also in the heading. F does the same in every account: it reads them all, so it is run only when you ask, and the box then lists the hits with their accounts. Enter on a hit goes to that entry in its account. Esc takes the find and the filter off. F5 reads again.
This stage only reads: the page changes no record and runs no command.
